## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of provider aliases and per-environment module instantiation in Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases and module-level provider mappings
- For_each environment deployment and target verification
- Safe plan/apply separation per environment

## Code Example
```javascript
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"dev\"
  region = \"us-west-2\"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"prod\"
  region = \"us-east-1\"
}
variable \"environments\" { type = \"map(string)\"; default = { prod = \"prod\"; staging = \"staging\" } }

module \"vpc\" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = \"./modules/vpc\"
  providers = {
    aws = each.key == \"prod\" ? aws.prod : aws.dev
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback if prod plan uses the wrong alias?
- How can you enforce alias usage at plan time to prevent mix-ups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of remote state isolation, cross-env data sharing, and basic CI gating for beginner Terraform workflows.

## Key Concepts
- terraform_remote_state data source
- backend alias and per-env state keys
- var-files for environment specificity (qa.tfvars)
- CI gating with plan validation before apply

## Code Example
```javascript
// qa backend example (HCL in Terraform syntax, shown as JS for formatting)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state"
    key            = "envs/qa/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tf-locks"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "prod" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "my-terraform-state"
    key    = "envs/prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multi-region VPCs?
- How would you test for drift between prod and qa after apply?
